Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree

LeetCode - Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree

My Understanding

What makes this problem beautiful is that the solution becomes simple only after understanding what an ancestor and a lowest common ancestor actually mean.

An ancestor is a node that exists above another node in the tree.

A common ancestor of p and q is a node that is an ancestor of both.

The lowest common ancestor is the common ancestor closest to p and q.

The Realization

I first reduced the problem to very simple tree shapes.

If the tree is:

      1
     / \
    2   3

and p = 2, q = 3, then 1 is the LCA because one node is found on the left and the other on the right.

Then I noticed that the same idea works for more complicated shapes.

There are essentially three situations:

1. One node is found on each side
        1
       / \
      p   q

Both subtrees contain one target.

Therefore, the current node is the point where their paths meet.

So the current node is the LCA.

2. Both nodes are in the same subtree
        1
       /
      2
     / \
    p   q

The left subtree contains both nodes.

So 1 cannot be the lowest common ancestor.

We continue searching inside that subtree.

3. One target is the current node
        p
         \
          q

If the current node itself is p or q, it can be the LCA.

Where Recursion Makes It Beautiful

The recursive function doesn't need to explicitly search for every possible tree shape.

Instead, every node asks:

"What did my left subtree find?"

and

"What did my right subtree find?"

The answers then naturally create the three cases.

              current
              /     \
         left answer  right answer

If both sides return something, then one target was found on each side.

Therefore:

left != null && right != null
        ↓
   current is LCA

If only one side returns something, that answer is passed upward.

left != null && right == null
        ↓
        left

or

left == null && right != null
        ↓
        right

If the current node is p or q, it returns itself.

The Core Insight

The recursion is not magically "finding the LCA."

It is simply allowing every subtree to report information to its parent.

Each subtree answers:

"Did I find either of the two target nodes?"

As these answers travel upward, eventually a node receives useful information from both sides.

That node is exactly where the two paths meet.

Therefore, it is the Lowest Common Ancestor.

Why I Like This Problem

The final implementation is surprisingly small compared to the level of understanding required to arrive at it.

The challenge isn't really writing the recursion.

The challenge is realizing that:

If every node asks its left and right subtree for their answers, the three possible outcomes already contain the entire solution.

That is what makes this problem so satisfying.

A simple recursive pattern ends up solving what initially looks like a complicated tree problem.

Complexity
Time: O(n) — every node may be visited once.
Space: O(h) — due to the recursion stack, where h is the height of the tree.